@page "/guide"
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@implements IDisposable

<PageTitle>German tenses explained · Konjugieren</PageTitle>

<article class="guide">
    <header class="guide-head">
        <h1>German tenses explained</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            What each mood and tense is for, how the regular forms are built, and a few examples.
            Every table on a verb page links here.
        </p>
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
            <a href="guide#basics">The basics</a>
            @foreach (var mood in Moods)
            {
                <a href="guide#@mood.Id">@mood.Name</a>
            }
            <a href="guide#infinitiv">Infinitive and participles</a>
            <a href="guide#trennbar">Separable verbs</a>
            <a href="guide#reflexiv">Reflexive verbs</a>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <section id="basics" class="guide-section">
        <h2>The basics</h2>
        <dl class="facts">
            <dt>Weak, strong and mixed verbs</dt>
            <dd>
                <strong>Weak</strong> verbs are the regular ones: the stem stays the same and the past adds <em lang="de">-te</em>
                (<em lang="de">machen → machte, gemacht</em>). Most German verbs are weak, and every new one is
                (<em lang="de">googeln, chatten</em>). <strong>Strong</strong> verbs change their vowel in the past and end the
                past participle in <em lang="de">-en</em> (<em lang="de">fahren → fuhr, gefahren</em>; <em lang="de">singen → sang, gesungen</em>).
                Wiktionary sorts them into seven classes by their vowel pattern. <strong>Mixed</strong> verbs change the vowel but take the weak
                endings (<em lang="de">bringen → brachte, gebracht</em>; <em lang="de">denken, kennen, wissen</em>). The modal verbs
                (<em lang="de">können, müssen, dürfen, mögen, sollen, wollen</em>) and <em lang="de">wissen</em> are the
                "preterite-present" verbs: their present looks like a past tense (<em lang="de">ich kann, er kann</em>, no <em>-t</em>).
            </dd>

            <dt>Six persons</dt>
            <dd>
                <em lang="de">ich</em> (I), <em lang="de">du</em> (you), <em lang="de">er/sie/es</em> (he/she/it), <em lang="de">wir</em> (we),
                <em lang="de">ihr</em> (you, plural) and <em lang="de">sie</em> (they). The polite <em lang="de">Sie</em> ("you", to one person or
                several) takes the same form as <em lang="de">sie</em> (they): <em lang="de">Sie machen</em>. Unlike Italian or Spanish,
                German always says the pronoun.
            </dd>

            <dt>Simple and compound tenses</dt>
            <dd>
                Only the present and the simple past (and their subjunctives) are one word. The other tenses are built with an auxiliary:
                <em lang="de">haben</em> or <em lang="de">sein</em> + past participle for the perfect tenses
                (<em lang="de">ich habe gemacht, ich bin gegangen</em>), and <em lang="de">werden</em> + infinitive for the future
                (<em lang="de">ich werde machen</em>). In a main clause the participle or infinitive goes to the end of the sentence:
                <em lang="de">Ich habe gestern einen Kuchen gebacken.</em>
            </dd>

            <dt><span lang="de">Haben</span> or <span lang="de">sein</span>?</dt>
            <dd>
                Most verbs use <em lang="de">haben</em>, including every verb with a direct object and every reflexive verb. <em lang="de">Sein</em>
                is used with verbs of movement from one place to another (<em lang="de">gehen, fahren, fliegen, kommen</em>), with changes of state
                (<em lang="de">werden, wachsen, sterben, einschlafen</em>), and with <em lang="de">sein, bleiben</em> and <em lang="de">passieren</em>.
                Some verbs take both: <em lang="de">ich bin nach Berlin gefahren</em> (I went to Berlin) but <em lang="de">ich habe das Auto gefahren</em>
                (I drove the car). The tables show these as <em lang="de">habe/bin gefahren</em>. In southern Germany, Austria and Switzerland
                <em lang="de">sein</em> is also used with <em lang="de">liegen, sitzen, stehen</em>.
            </dd>

            <dt>Spelling changes that are still regular</dt>
            <dd>
                Stems ending in <em>-d</em>, <em>-t</em> or a consonant + <em>m/n</em> add an <em>e</em> before endings that start with a consonant
                (<em lang="de">arbeiten → du arbeitest, er arbeitet, arbeitete</em>; <em lang="de">atmen → er atmet</em>). Stems ending in
                <em>s, ß, x, z</em> take only <em>-t</em> for <em>du</em> (<em lang="de">du reist, du heißt</em>). Verbs in <em lang="de">-eln</em> and
                <em lang="de">-ern</em> end the infinitive and the <em>wir/sie</em> forms in <em>-n</em>, and <em lang="de">-eln</em> verbs usually drop
                an <em>e</em> for <em>ich</em> (<em lang="de">sammeln → ich sammle, wir sammeln</em>). Verbs in <em lang="de">-ieren</em> and verbs with
                an unstressed prefix have no <em>ge-</em> in the past participle (<em lang="de">studiert, besucht, verkauft</em>).
            </dd>

            <dt>What the red forms mean</dt>
            <dd>
                Konjugieren builds the weak form for every slot and compares it with the real form. Forms that differ are shown in
                <span class="irr">red</span>, so a strong verb shows exactly where its vowel changes (<em lang="de">du fährst, er fährt</em>, all of
                <em lang="de">fuhr</em>, <em lang="de">gefahren</em>) and the rest stays black. The spelling changes above don't count as irregular.
                A weak verb whose forms are almost all red gets a warning: the source table is probably wrong.
            </dd>

            <dt>Main and subordinate clauses</dt>
            <dd>
                In a main clause the conjugated verb is the second element (<em lang="de">Ich rufe dich morgen an.</em>). After
                <em lang="de">dass, weil, wenn, ob, als</em> and relative pronouns it goes to the end, and a separable verb is written as one word again:
                <em lang="de">…, dass ich dich morgen anrufe</em>; <em lang="de">…, weil ich ihn angerufen habe</em>. The
                <em>Subordinate clause</em> switch on a verb page shows every tense in that order.
            </dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    @foreach (var mood in Moods)
    {
        <section id="@mood.Id" class="guide-section">
            <h2>@mood.Name <small>@mood.English</small></h2>
            <p class="mood-intro">@((MarkupString)mood.Intro)</p>

            @foreach (var t in mood.Tenses)
            {
                <div id="@t.Id" class="tense-guide @(t.Compound ? "compound" : "")">
                    <h3>@t.Name <small>@t.English</small></h3>
                    <p><strong>Used for:</strong> @((MarkupString)t.Usage)</p>
                    <p><strong>Formed:</strong> @((MarkupString)t.Formation)</p>
                    @if (t.Endings is not null)
                    {
                        <table class="endings">
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th></th><th lang="de">machen</th><th lang="de">arbeiten</th><th lang="de">fahren</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                @for (var p = 0; p < t.Endings.Length; p++)
                                {
                                    <tr>
                                        <th scope="row" lang="de">@t.Pronouns[p]</th>
                                        @foreach (var form in t.Endings[p])
                                        {
                                            <td lang="de">@Stem(form)<b>@Ending(form)</b></td>
                                        }
                                    </tr>
                                }
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    }
                    <ul class="examples">
                        @foreach (var (de, en) in t.Examples)
                        {
                            <li><span lang="de">@de</span> <span class="en">@en</span></li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                    @if (Links.TryGetValue(t.Id, out var verbs))
                    {
                        <p class="see">See it in: @foreach (var v in verbs) { <a href="verb/@v" lang="de">@v</a><span class="comma">, </span> }</p>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </section>
    }

    <section id="infinitiv" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Infinitive and participles <small>infinite Verbformen</small></h2>
        <p class="mood-intro">These forms don't change for the person.</p>

        <div class="tense-guide">
            <h3>Infinitiv <small>infinitive</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> the dictionary form; after modal verbs (<em lang="de">ich kann schwimmen</em>), with
                <em lang="de">werden</em> for the future, and as a noun (<em lang="de">das Essen</em>). The
                <em lang="de">Infinitiv Perfekt</em> (<em lang="de">gemacht haben, gegangen sein</em>) is the infinitive of a completed action:
                <em lang="de">Er muss es vergessen haben.</em>
            </p>
        </div>

        <div id="zu-infinitiv" class="tense-guide">
            <h3>zu-Infinitiv <small>infinitive with zu</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> after most verbs other than modals, and after <em lang="de">um, ohne, statt</em>. Separable verbs
                take the <em lang="de">zu</em> between the prefix and the verb, as one word: <em lang="de">anzurufen, aufzustehen</em>.
            </p>
            <ul class="examples">
                <li><span lang="de">Ich habe vergessen, dich anzurufen.</span> <span class="en">I forgot to call you.</span></li>
                <li><span lang="de">Er lernt Deutsch, um in Berlin zu arbeiten.</span> <span class="en">He's learning German in order to work in Berlin.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="partizip-i" class="tense-guide">
            <h3>Partizip I <small>present participle</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> mostly as an adjective, with adjective endings: <em lang="de">ein lachendes Kind</em> (a laughing child),
                <em lang="de">die steigenden Preise</em>. Formed as infinitive + <em>-d</em>: <em lang="de">lachend, schlafend</em>. German has no
                continuous tense: "I am reading" is just <em lang="de">ich lese</em>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div id="partizip-ii" class="tense-guide">
            <h3>Partizip II <small>past participle</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> all the perfect tenses, the passive (<em lang="de">werden</em> + participle:
                <em lang="de">das Haus wird gebaut</em>), and as an adjective (<em lang="de">ein gekochtes Ei</em>). Weak verbs:
                <em>ge-</em> + stem + <em>-t</em> (<em lang="de">gemacht, gearbeitet</em>). Strong verbs: <em>ge-</em> + stem, often with another vowel,
                + <em>-en</em> (<em lang="de">gefahren, gesungen, geschrieben</em>). No <em>ge-</em> after an unstressed prefix or in
                <em lang="de">-ieren</em> verbs (<em lang="de">besucht, verstanden, studiert</em>); separable verbs put it after the prefix
                (<em lang="de">angerufen, eingekauft</em>).
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="trennbar" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Separable verbs <small>trennbare Verben</small></h2>
        <p>
            Many verbs start with a stressed prefix that splits off in a main clause and goes to the end: <em lang="de">anrufen → ich rufe dich an</em>,
            <em lang="de">aufstehen → ich stehe um sieben auf</em>, <em lang="de">einkaufen, mitkommen, zurückgehen</em>. The prefix stays attached in the
            infinitive, in the participles (<em lang="de">angerufen</em>, with <em>ge-</em> in the middle), in the <em lang="de">zu</em>-infinitive
            (<em lang="de">anzurufen</em>) and in a subordinate clause (<em lang="de">…, wenn ich anrufe</em>). The inseparable prefixes
            <em lang="de">be-, emp-, ent-, er-, ge-, miss-, ver-, zer-</em> never split and take no <em>ge-</em> (<em lang="de">besuchen → besucht</em>).
        </p>
        <p class="see">See it in: <a href="verb/anrufen" lang="de">anrufen</a>, <a href="verb/aufstehen" lang="de">aufstehen</a>, <a href="verb/einkaufen" lang="de">einkaufen</a>, <a href="verb/wiedergutmachen" lang="de">wiedergutmachen</a></p>
    </section>

    <section id="reflexiv" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Reflexive verbs <small>reflexive Verben</small></h2>
        <p>
            Reflexive verbs come with <em lang="de">sich</em> in the dictionary (<em lang="de">sich freuen, sich beeilen</em>) and use the pronouns
            <em lang="de">mich, dich, sich, uns, euch, sich</em>: <em lang="de">ich freue mich, du freust dich</em>. Their perfect tenses take
            <em lang="de">haben</em>: <em lang="de">ich habe mich gefreut</em>. Many ordinary verbs can also be used reflexively
            (<em lang="de">waschen → sich waschen</em>); Wiktionary lists those under the plain verb, which is why the dictionary has only a few
            entries starting with <em lang="de">sich</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="see">See it in: <a href="verb/sich%20sputen" lang="de">sich sputen</a>, <a href="verb/sich%20akklimatisieren" lang="de">sich akklimatisieren</a></p>
    </section>
</article>

@code {
